    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Holafly's code is ours; Saily doesn't run the kind of affiliate program that supports a code like that, so its code is the best public one we've verified, applying storewide rather than being limited to first-time buyers.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Holafly or Saily the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Holafly if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You genuinely don't know how much data you'll use</li>
        <li>Heavy streaming, video calls, or hotspot use</li>
        <li>China is on the itinerary and you want the built-in VPN</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Saily if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You'd normally pay for a VPN or ad-blocker separately when traveling anyway</li>
        <li>Multi-country trip where you don't want a new eSIM at every border</li>
        <li>Light data user who'd rather pay per GB than a flat unlimited rate</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Holafly's China eSIM has a VPN built in; Saily's security tools don't cover China the same way.</strong> Holafly's China plan lets Google Maps, WhatsApp, and Gmail work without installing anything extra. Saily's bundled tools are ad and malware blocking plus a virtual-location switch, not a China-specific bypass, so a Saily user still needs a separate solution for China.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Both were hit by the same Turkey purchase block.</strong> Saily and Holafly are both among the eSIM apps whose sites and apps were blocked by Turkey's telecom regulator (BTK) starting mid-2025. On either provider, an eSIM bought and installed before you land keeps working; you just can't buy or top up while physically inside the country.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Holafly vs Saily FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Holafly or Saily</h3>
      <p>Depends on usage. Saily is usually cheaper for light data use on a short trip. Holafly is usually cheaper, or at least less stressful, for heavy data use since the flat price doesn't change no matter how much is used.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either come with a VPN</h3>
      <p>Saily bundles ad blocking, malware/phishing blocking, and a virtual-location tool into every plan as standard. Holafly's China-specific eSIM includes a built-in VPN just for that destination; its other plans don't include one.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I use one eSIM across multiple countries</h3>
      <p>Saily's is designed for that: one universal eSIM keeps working as you cross into a new supported country. Holafly plans are scoped to the specific country or region purchased.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Do Holafly and Saily work in Turkey</h3>
      <p>Both had their websites and apps blocked by Turkey's telecom regulator starting mid-2025. An eSIM bought and installed before arrival keeps working on either provider; neither can be purchased or topped up while physically inside Turkey.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

